## 1. Introduction
The development of clean and renewable energy sources such as wind energy and solar energy to replace traditional fossil fuels has become the key to solving current energy demand and relating
environmental problems. Due to the intermittent and unstable characteristics of most of such renewable energy, high-efficiency and low-cost energy storage technology has become an indispensable key technical support for the utilization of these energy sources [1–3]. Among various energy storage devices, hybrid supercapacitors, as a novel system integrated with merits from battery and supercapacitors, have drawn great attention owing to their high power and energy density [4–6]. But until now, the property of electrode materials is still being regarded as one of the key factors determines the development of HSCs [7–9]. Therefore, the exploration and design of electrode materials with high specific capacity and long cycle life have become a hot research topic.
In recent years, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as promising battery-type positive electrode for HSCs due to their high specific surface area and variable sites for redox reactions [10–12]. However, the low conductivity and weak chemical stability of most MOFs greatly hinder the effective utilization of built-in redox centers and against the long-term cycling performance. Recently, interfacial engineering has been regarded as an effective strategy to boost the electrochemical performances of materials. It is reported that the formed interface between different components can be used as a channel to promote electron transfer from one to another components and tune the electronic state, which not only improves the electron conductivity but also enhances the stability and reactivity, benefiting the practical applications [13–19]. Although the construction of composite materials by combining MOFs with other substrates such as polymer, graphene oxide and acetylene black has been developed to improve their capacity performance [20–22], most of them are linked by weak van der Waals forces and lack strong coupled bonds between the MOFs and substrates, which still restricts the effective interfacial charge-transfer capacity and desirable stability protection of MOFs.
Bearing this in mind, we paid our attentions to a family of new two-dimension early transition metal carbides, nitrides/carbonitrides, labeled as MXenes, which are synthesized by etching away the A layers from MAX phase [23–26]. Benefiting from the metallic conductivity and rich surface functional groups such as the OH, O and F caused by the synthesis procedure, MXenes have shown great potential to act as a fascinating substrate [27]. Moreover, the negatively charged surface groups of MXenes could be favorable for the formation of composites with strong interfacial coupled bonds at the interface, thereby affects the electronic structures of the supported materials [28–30]. Therefore, MXenes are indeed ideal substrates to amend the shortcomings of MOFs and create better electrochemical performances.
Herein, we report an thermal treatment strategy to design strongly coupled composite materials with hybrid nanosheets as highly efficient positive electrodes for HSCs, in which the CoMOF nanosheets are closely anchored on MXene nanosheets. Prominently, the annealing treatment at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) can increase the strength of interfacial interactions between Co-BDC and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) without the change of crystalline structure and maintains the nanosheet morphology. The strong interfacial interactions modulate the electronic structures of Co-BDC and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\), and results in the electron transfer from \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) to Co-BDC in the hybrid nanosheets, which is favorable for improving overall conductivity and electrochemical reactivity. As expected, the CoTC hybrid nanosheets annealed at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (denoted as CoTC-300) achieve a high specific capacity of \(1453~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2}\) \((217.3~\mathrm{C~cm}^{-3})\) at \(2~\mathrm{A~m}^{-2}\), which is much higher than that of its counterparts. Moreover, the fabricated CoTC-300//AC hybrid supercapacitor exhibits a high energy density of 176.3 Wh \(\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) at the power density of \(337.5~\mathrm{W~L}^{-1}\) and excellent cycling performance. This work paves a new route for developing advanced MOF-based electrode materials for HSCs.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and characterization
All chemicals are analytical reagents and can be used directly as received without further purification. Cobalt chloride hexahydrate \(\left(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\), terephthalic acid (BDC), N, N-Dimethyl form amide (DMF), ethanol, acetone and FTO were purchased from Alfa Aesar. \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}\) powders were purchased from Keene Ceramic Materials Co., Ltd. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was performed on a Rigaku Ultima IV diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation \((\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~})\). X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were acquired on an ESCALAB250XI. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was taken on JEOL F200. Thermogravimetric (TG) curves were conducted on a NETZSCH 449C thermal analyzer.
## 2.2. Materials synthesis
Preparation of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) nanosheets: The layered \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) was first synthesized by HF etching of the pristine \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}\). In detail, \(1.0~\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}\) powers were slowly added into \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) \((\sim\!40\%)\) HF solution in a \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined, and the suspension was constantly stirred at \(70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2~\mathrm{h}\). Then, the black suspension was carefully washed and centrifuged by distilled water for several times until the pH of the solution reached 6–7, finally dried at room temperature under vacuum. Secondly, \(80~\mathrm{mg}\) layered \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) was dispersed into \(80~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF solution and ultrasonicated for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) under argon atmosphere. Afterward, the resultant dispersion was collected after the centrifugation at about \(3000~\mathrm{rpm}\) for later use.
Preparation of Co-BDC/\(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) (CoTC) hybrid nanosheets: In detail, \(0.178~\mathrm{g}\) \(\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) was added into \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) \((32~\mathrm{mL}, 1~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL}^{-1})\) DMF dispersion under stirring for \(20~\mathrm{min}\). Then, \(0.125~\mathrm{g}\) BDC, \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol and \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water were added into the as-prepared \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) DMF dispersion under stirring for another \(10~\mathrm{min}\). Subsequently, the mixture was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated at \(140~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(48~\mathrm{h}\). After cooling to room temperature naturally, the resulting CoTC precipitate was collected by centrifuging and washed several times with DMF and ethanol, and dried at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a vacuum oven. Preparation of Co-BDC/\(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) 300 \({}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (CoTC-300) hybrid nanosheets: CoTC-300 was synthesized by directly heating the CoTC in a tube furnace at \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1~\mathrm{h}\) with a ramp rate of \(2~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) under the argon atmosphere.
Preparation of Co-BDC: The preparation process was the same as that of CoTC without the addition of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\).
## 2.3. Electrochemical measurements
The electrochemical measurements were conducted with a standard three-electrode and two-electrode systems in \(1.0~\mathrm{M}\) KOH electrolyte by using CHI 660E Electrochemical Workstation. The techniques used to diagnose the electrochemical performance were cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostic charge–discharge (GCD) and impedance. In this work, FTO acts as the current collector. For working electrode, \(3~\mathrm{mg}\) as-obtained sample was dispersed into \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) water under ultrasonication for \(10~\mathrm{min}\). Then, the dispersion was vacuum-filtrated over a cellulose membrane with \(0.22~\mathrm{um}\) pore size to form a homogeneous thin film, which was immediately transferred onto the FTO [31]. The thickness can be tuned by modulating the amount of water. \(\mathrm{Hg/HgO}\) electrode and platinum foil were used as the reference and counter electrode, respectively. The area \((\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{m}},\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{m}^{2})\) and volume specific capacity \((\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{v}},\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{cm}^{3})\) of the sample can be calculated from the GCD curve using the following equations: \(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{m}}=1~\Delta\mathrm{t}/\mathrm{A}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{v}}=1\Delta\mathrm{t}/\mathrm{V}\), where I is the constant discharge current, \(\Delta\mathrm{t}\) represents the discharge time, A is the elec
trode area and V represents the electrode volume, respectively. For a hybrid supercapacitor device test, the HSC was assembled by using CoTC-300 and active carbon (AC) as the positive and negative electrode, respectively. Moreover, the positive and negative electrode should be balanced to follow the relationship \( \mathsf{Q}_{+} = \mathsf{Q}_{-} \). The energy density (E, Wh/L) and power density (P, W/L) can be calculated according to the following equations: \( \mathrm{E} = 1/2\mathrm{C}\Delta\mathrm{V}^{2} \), \( \mathrm{P} = \mathrm{E}/\Delta\mathrm{t} \), where C refers to the volume specific capacity, \( \Delta\mathrm{V} \) (V) represents the voltage window, \( \Delta\mathrm{t} \) (s) is the discharge time, respectively.
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthetic procedure of CoTC-300 hybrid nanosheets is presented in Fig. 1, which involves the preparation of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) nanosheets, followed via a simple two-step approach including solvothermal reaction and low-temperature annealing (see experimental section for details). Briefly, \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) nanosheets were fabricated by a combined process of HF etching and liquid exfoliation. Subsequently, \( \mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \) was introduced into the prefabricated \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3} \)-\( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) nanosheets dispersion, in which their rich termination groups can easily adsorb \( \mathrm{Co}^{2+} \) ions. Benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC) was then added into the above suspension for solvothermal reaction to obtain \( \mathrm{Co-BDC}/\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) hybrid nanosheets. Finally, the hybrid nanosheets were subjected to the heat treatment in Ar atmosphere at \( 300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for one hour and the obtained product was labeled as CoTC-300.
Detailed phase and composition analyses of the products were performed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Fig. 2a, the XRD pattern of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2} \) (JCPDS Card No. 52-0875) demonstrates its high crystallinity and purity. Compared with the \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2} \) precursor, the disappearance of the strongest (1 0 4) peak at \( 39^{\circ} \) of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) and the (0 0 2) peak shifts from \( 9.5^{\circ} \) \( (\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2}) \) to \( 6.9^{\circ} \) \( (\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}) \) indicate the successful removal of the Al layer from \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{AlC}_{2} \) precursor by HF etching [28,32]. Meanwhile, the XRD pattern of the synthesized Co-BDC is matched well with the simulated one (No. 985792) [33]. For the CoTC and CoTC-300, their diffraction peaks are almost the same, which can be assigned to the Co-BDC, implying that there is no phase change after low-temperature annealing. The inductively coupled plasma (ICP) reveals the hybrid level of approximately \( 37.15\,\mathrm{wt\%} \) Co and \( 2.73\,\mathrm{wt\%} \) Ti in CoTC-300. The low loading amount and the relatively poor diffraction intensity of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3} \)-\( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) are responsible for the absence of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) diffraction peaks in hybrid nanosheets. Nonetheless, the presence of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) in CoTC-300 can be confirmed by XPS. Fig. S1a shows the XPS survey spectrum of CoTC-300, from which the Ti 2p, Co 2p, C 1s and O 1s peaks are detected. For the \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) and two composites, the valence of Ti 2p is contributed by four different valences of Ti (I), Ti (II), Ti (III) and Ti (IV) (Fig. 2b), where Ti (IV) belongs to oxygen-containing groups attached on the surface of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) [28]. Compared with \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3} \)-\( \mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \), an obvious shift, particularly for Ti (III) to the higher binding energy is observed in the CoTC and CoTC-300. Moreover, the peaks corresponding to Ti (I) and Ti (II) become weaker, while peaks corresponding to Ti (III) became remarkable stronger. Both the results show that the valence and binding energy of Ti in the composites increased. It should be noted that the peaks belong to Ti-F bonds disappear, and it is in accordance with the absence of F 1s spectrum in the hybrid nanosheets (Fig. S1b). This phenomenon could be attributed to the F groups being replaced by oxygen-containing groups (-O or -OH) in the aqueous environment during the hydrothermal/solvothermal reaction [34].
To further confirm the chemical state of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) in hybrid nanosheets, XPS spectra of CoTC-300 and CoTC with etching depth of \(75\,\mathrm{nm}\) was studied after surface etching with Ar plasma. With an etching depth of \(75\,\textrm{n m}\) , four different Ti valences can also be detected, which is the signature of C-Ti-C structure in \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\)
To further confirm the chemical state of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) in hybrid nanosheets, XPS spectra of CoTC-300 and CoTC with etching depth of \( 75\,\mathrm{nm} \) was studied after surface etching with Ar plasma. With an etching depth of \( 75\,\mathrm{nm} \), four different Ti valences can also be detected, which is the signature of C-Ti-C structure in \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) (Fig. 2c), further indicating that the outer surface and the inner crystalline structure of \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \) are well preserved. Moreover, changes in peak intensity and binding energy in two composites are further confirmed compared with \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \). Besides, it can be clearly seen that the binding energy of Ti 2p for CoTC-300 has a small positive shift compared with that of CoTC. Meanwhile, the Co \( 2\mathrm{p}_{3/2} \) and Co \( 2\mathrm{p}_{1/2} \) of CoTC and CoTC-300 has a large negative shift relative to the Co-BDC (Fig. 2d). The positive shift in the XPS binding energy indicates that a reduction in charge density, while the negative shift corresponds to an increase in charge density around the atom. Therefore, the shifted XPS peaks of Ti 2p and Co 2p for the hybrid nanosheets suggest that a portion of the electrons transfer from Ti to Co ions. Moreover, in the XPS O 1s spectra (Fig. 2e), except for the Ti-O peak at \( 529.9\,\mathrm{eV} \) of CoTC and CoTC-300, a new peak at \( 530.7\,\mathrm{eV} \) belongs to the Ti-O-Co bonds can be clearly found in the two hybrid nanosheets, which is similar to the XPS results in the reported literature [28,35]. Furthermore, the peak area of the Ti-O-Co bonds for CoTC-300 is larger than that of CoTC, indicating that low temperature heat treatment is beneficial to strengthen the interface interactions. X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) was further performed to give intrinsic structure information. As displayed in Fig. 2f, the energy peak of the Ti L-edge of the CoTC and CoTC-300 has a larger positive shift than that of the \( \mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x} \), while the energy peak of Co L-edge is shifted to a lower energy compared with that of the Co-BDC (Fig. S2). Moreover, the shift of energy peak for CoTC-300 is larger than that of CoTC. These results further demonstrate the enhanced electron transfer from Ti to Co species through the Ti-O-Co bonds in CoTC-300 hybrid nanosheets, which can lead to an abundance of electrons around the Co species, resulting in the enhanced conductivity of Co-BDC. Meanwhile, the Ti species that lose a partial electron may act as electrophiles to absorb the hydroxyl group in KOH solution, thereby improving the reactivity.
curves (Fig. 4a), the current density and integral area of the CoTC-300 are the largest, revealing that the specific capacity of CoTC-300 outperforms than other composites and single-component. Moreover, the specific capacity of the CoTC-300 calculated from the galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) curves (Fig. 4b) is \(1453~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2}\) \((217.3~\mathrm{C~cm}^{-3})\) at \(2~\mathrm{A~m}^{-2}\), which is remarkably higher than that of the \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) \((76.2~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2})\), Co-BDC \((316~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2})\), CoTC \((488~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2})\), CoTC-200 \((536~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2})\) and CoTC-400 \((868~\mathrm{C~m}^{-2})\). Obviously, the incorporation of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) nanosheets and followed annealing treatment at the optimized \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) result in the improved electrochemical performance of the hybrid nanosheets. These results can be attributed to the existence of strong interfacial interactions between Co-BDC and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) in CoTC-300, which is favorable for the electron transfer process, as confirmed by the
electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (ESI) (Fig. S3). Of note, when the temperature is lifted to \(400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), although the CoTC-400 composite doesn't decompose (TG in Fig. S4), the nanosheet morphology is destroyed (TEM in Fig. S5) and thus results in the lower capacity performance of CoTC-400 relative to the CoTC-300.
Fig. 4c displays the CV curves of the CoTC-300 hybrid nanosheets at the scan rates from 5 to \(50~\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}\), and similar CV curves at each scan rate are clearly presented, demonstrating the good rate capability and electrochemical reversibility. To reveal the charge storage mechanism of the CoTC-300 hybrid nanosheets, the cathodic peaks current as a function of square root of scan rate \((\mathbf{v}^{0.5})\) is presented (Fig. 4d) to indicate the diffusion-controlled charge storage, which is a typical characteristic of battery-type electrode material [37]. The GCD curves of the CoTC-300 at
different current densities are presented in Fig. 4e. All the GCD curves have the similar voltage platform and symmetrical characteristic, showing the high reversibility and Coulombic efficiency. The corresponding areal specific capacities of the CoTC-300 are 1456, 1380, 1300 and \(1233.5\;\mathrm{C}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\) at \(2{-}5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\), respectively, showing good rate performance. Besides, the electrochemical sta
bility of the electrode is an important parameter to assess the property. Thus, a long time GCD test was conducted to estimate the cycle stability. As displayed in Fig. 4f, the specific capacity retains \(92\%\) of its initial capacity and the Coulombic efficiency is still kept at \(98\%\) after 2500 cycles at \(5\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{m}^{-2}\), which means that it has good cyclic durability. Finally, the sample of CoTC-300 after
long time GCD test was collected and performed microscopic characterization. As illustrated in Fig. S6, the TEM images clearly show the ultrathin nanosheet morphology. Furthermore, the corresponding HRTEM image (Fig. S7a) clearly shows two lattice fringes with d-spacing of 0.23 and \(0.36\;\mathrm{nm}\), which exclusive belong to the plane of Co-BDC at about \(20 = 37.5^{\circ}\) and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) at about \(20 = 25.2^{\circ}\), respectively. In addition, elemental mapping images show the existence and well distribution of Ti, Co, O and C in the whole nanosheets. All the above results confirm the co-existence of \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) and Co-BDC in CoTC-300 after long time GCD test.
In order to evaluate the feasibility of the practical application of CoTC-300, the electrochemical performance of the hybrid supercapacitor (HSC) was evaluated with CoTC-300 as the positive electrode, activated carbon (AC) as the negative electrode, and \(1.0\;\mathrm{M}\) KOH as the electrolyte. In order to obtain the best voltage window for electrochemical measurement, the CV curve of the HSC device was measured under various voltage windows. As shown in Fig. 5a, when the voltage window is \(0{-}1.5\;\mathrm{V}\), the CV curve has reached its ideal state. Moreover, as the scan rate increases, the current response becomes more obvious. Even at the scan rate of \(50\;\mathrm{mV\;s}^{-1}\), the shape of the CV curve is maintained well (Fig. 5b), indicating its good rate performance and fast current response. Fig. 5c shows the GCD curves of the HSC device at different current densities. It could be found that the voltage discharge platform is not obvious and the capacity value was calculated to be \(3780\) \((564.2\;\mathrm{C\;cm}^{-3})\), \(3564\) \((532\;\mathrm{C\;cm}^{-3})\) and \(3408\) \((508.7\;\mathrm{C\;cm}^{-3})\) \(\mathrm{C\;m}^{-2}\) at a current density of \(2{-}4\;\mathrm{A\;m}^{-2}\), respectively, giving good capacity performance. Moreover, the HSC device presents a high volume energy density of \(176.3\), \(166.2\) and \(158.9\;\mathrm{Wh\;L}^{-1}\) at the power density to be \(337.5\), \(498.6\) and \(670.4\;\mathrm{W\;L}^{-1}\), respectively. These results indicate bright potential
of the synthesized CoTC-300 hybrid nanosheets in high energy HSC. The cycling performance of the CoTC-300//AC HSC was studied at a high current density of \(10\;\mathrm{A\;m}^{-2}\), for which the result is displayed in Fig. 5d. It can be seen that the CoTC-300//AC HSC device demonstrates excellent cycling durability with about \(100\%\) of the initial specific capacity even after 5000 cycles. It should be noted that the first fluctuation of the stability curve could be attributed to the penetration of electrolyte solution into the interior of the electrode material and the participation of more active sites in the surface redox reaction for energy storage, resulting in a gradual increase in the capacity. When the permeation reaches a maximum, the increase in active sites can no longer compensate for the decline in capacity, so the capacity begins to gradually decrease. On the other side, the latter fluctuations could be ascribed to the change of ion transport efficiency caused by the large temperature difference between day and night during the test, which leads to the regular change of capacity [38,39]. Besides, the leakage current drops significantly to \(33.42\;\upmu\mathrm{A}\) within a few seconds, and then remains constant (Fig. S8a). The self-discharge characteristic of this device maintain the voltage of \(0.6\;\mathrm{V}\) of its initial voltage \((1.5\;\mathrm{V})\) after \(25\;\mathrm{h}\) self-discharge (Fig. S8b), which indicates the HSC device is in good health. All the results above confirm the excellent electrochemical performance of CoTC-300//AC HSC.
## 4. Conclusion
To summarize, we have discovered that a simple thermal treatment at \(300\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) of the \(\mathrm{Co-BDC/Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) (denoted as CoTC-300) can achieve greatly improved capacity performance without the change of crystalline structure and morphology. Detailed characterization confirms that more Co-O-Ti bridging bonds are
constructed at the interface between the Co-BDC and \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) of CoTC-300 compared with that of \(\mathrm{Co-BDC}/\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) (denoted as CoTC). The strong coupled chemical bonds act as a channel to accelerate the electron transfer from \(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x}\) to Co-BDC, and tune their electronic states, which not only could improve the overall electron conductivity but also enhance the stability and reactivity. As expected, the CoTC-300 hybrid nanosheets show significant enhanced specific capacity of \(217.3~\mathrm{C~cm}^{-3}\) at \(2~\mathrm{A~m}^{-2}\), which is much higher than that of their counterparts and also comparable to previously reported related works [3,40–42]. Moreover, the fabricated hybrid supercapacitor with the CoTC-300 as the positive electrode and active carbon as the negative electrode exhibits a high energy density and good stability. This work focuses on the design of strong interfacial interactions between MOF and MXene for the application of HSCs. We believe that this method can also be applied to other composite systems, and more high-performance materials will be designed for energy storage and conversion in the future.
